1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,205
Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:03,205 --> 00:00:05,349
If you're new here, welcome.

3
00:00:05,349 --> 00:00:10,424
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

4
00:00:11,046 --> 00:00:16,355
This year, foreign investors have pulled nearly twice as much money out of Indian

5
00:00:16,355 --> 00:00:19,089
shares as they did in the whole of last year.

6
00:00:19,089 --> 00:00:21,306
Why are they leaving, and does it matter?

7
00:00:21,988 --> 00:00:23,028
Hi, I'm Mittal Shah.

8
00:00:23,028 --> 00:00:29,350
This is an AI-generated version of my voice, and it is narrating today's story.

9
00:00:30,213 --> 00:00:31,493
First, who are we talking about?

10
00:00:31,493 --> 00:00:37,680
Foreign portfolio investors, or F-P-Is, are overseas funds and institutions that buy

11
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:40,097
shares and bonds in Indian companies.

12
00:00:40,097 --> 00:00:41,802
They do not run the business.

13
00:00:41,802 --> 00:00:43,685
They simply hold a slice of it.

14
00:00:43,685 --> 00:00:47,284
When they sell more than they buy, that is called an outflow.

15
00:00:47,897 --> 00:00:53,378
So far in 2026, F-P-Is have taken about three point one lakh crore rupees out of

16
00:00:53,378 --> 00:00:55,017
Indian shares.

17
00:00:55,017 --> 00:00:59,864
Last year, the whole-year figure was one point six six lakh crore.

18
00:00:59,864 --> 00:01:05,202
And October alone has added around fifty-two thousand crore rupees, counted up to

19
00:01:05,202 --> 00:01:06,258
about the eighteenth.

20
00:01:06,864 --> 00:01:08,903
It has not been a straight line.

21
00:01:08,903 --> 00:01:12,149
Foreign investors were buyers in July and August.

22
00:01:12,149 --> 00:01:17,642
And provisional figures suggest they turned net buyers again on the sixteenth of

23
00:01:17,642 --> 00:01:20,530
October, ending a run of fifteen sessions of selling.

24
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:23,896
But the year as a whole is heavily negative.

25
00:01:24,499 --> 00:01:25,928
Why are they selling?

26
00:01:25,928 --> 00:01:30,310
Analysts point to four things, and most of them are outside India.

27
00:01:30,735 --> 00:01:33,308
First, American interest rates.

28
00:01:33,308 --> 00:01:38,381
The ten-year yield on United States government bonds has recently crossed five point

29
00:01:38,381 --> 00:01:39,023
two per cent.

30
00:01:39,023 --> 00:01:43,735
A yield is simply the interest an investor earns for lending to a government.

31
00:01:43,735 --> 00:01:48,297
When the American government pays that much, a safe dollar bond starts to look

32
00:01:48,297 --> 00:01:50,439
better than a riskier Indian share.

33
00:01:51,079 --> 00:01:52,804
Second, oil.

34
00:01:52,804 --> 00:01:56,346
Brent crude has hovered around a hundred dollars a barrel.

35
00:01:56,346 --> 00:02:00,576
India buys most of its oil from abroad, so dear oil raises costs and can squeeze

36
00:02:00,576 --> 00:02:02,217
company profits.

37
00:02:02,684 --> 00:02:03,801
Third, the currency.

38
00:02:03,801 --> 00:02:06,960
A foreign investor earns in rupees but counts in dollars.

39
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:10,790
If the rupee weakens, the same shares are worth fewer dollars.

40
00:02:11,215 --> 00:02:12,420
And fourth, price.

41
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:18,125
Indian shares remain expensive compared with how fast company earnings are growing.

42
00:02:18,125 --> 00:02:24,127
Meanwhile, a rally in artificial intelligence stocks in other Asian markets has been

43
00:02:24,127 --> 00:02:25,952
pulling money elsewhere.

44
00:02:26,546 --> 00:02:32,091
In Parliament in July, the finance ministry described the outflows as part of a

45
00:02:32,091 --> 00:02:37,870
wider pattern across emerging markets, not something specific to India.

46
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:43,636
It listed geopolitical tension, uncertainty over trade tariffs, currency moves, and

47
00:02:43,636 --> 00:02:45,993
global funds rebalancing their portfolios.

48
00:02:46,556 --> 00:02:47,778
So, a quick recap:

49
00:02:47,778 --> 00:02:53,145
foreign money is leaving, mostly because of American rates, oil and the dollar.

50
00:02:53,753 --> 00:02:57,567
Now, who has been on the other side of these sales?

51
00:02:57,567 --> 00:03:01,279
Domestic institutional investors, or D-I-Is.

52
00:03:01,279 --> 00:03:06,366
These are Indian mutual funds, insurers and similar institutions that invest

53
00:03:06,366 --> 00:03:07,260
Indians' savings.

54
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:12,622
They have put in more than five lakh crore rupees for the third calendar year in a

55
00:03:12,622 --> 00:03:13,222
row.

56
00:03:12,845 --> 00:03:16,710
In simple terms, Indian savings have been buying what foreigners sell.

57
00:03:17,468 --> 00:03:20,339
What does this mean in practice?

58
00:03:20,339 --> 00:03:26,076
One likely effect is that share prices swing more when a big group of sellers is

59
00:03:26,076 --> 00:03:28,315
active, though strong domestic buying has softened that.

60
00:03:28,315 --> 00:03:33,938
Another is pressure on the rupee, because foreign sellers convert rupees back into

61
00:03:33,938 --> 00:03:34,538
dollars.

62
00:03:34,382 --> 00:03:40,004
Both are inferences about how the market usually works, not figures from this data.

63
00:03:40,765 --> 00:03:42,577
What do we not know?

64
00:03:42,577 --> 00:03:47,301
The numbers differ depending on how they are counted, and whether you include new

65
00:03:47,301 --> 00:03:49,762
share issues.

66
00:03:49,762 --> 00:03:52,981
Flows can also reverse quickly, as the early signs this month show.

67
00:03:52,981 --> 00:03:55,771
Next month's data will tell us whether the selling is easing.

68
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:58,262
To sum up:

69
00:03:58,262 --> 00:04:03,891
foreign investors are leaving Indian shares mostly because of what is happening

70
00:04:03,891 --> 00:04:10,201
outside India, in American rates, oil and the dollar, and so far Indian savers have

71
00:04:10,201 --> 00:04:12,077
absorbed much of the selling.

72
00:04:12,963 --> 00:04:15,624
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode.

